From 7042c7caaa14945ce9654349d1e9207e677c2ebd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Garcia <44309076+ElGuslml@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 03:32:14 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 19/24] gpu: d3d12: keep color resolves as host render target
 copies (lazy resolves)

A resolve of a color render target to memory costs three or four
full-size passes before the game can sample the result: the render
target is dumped into the EDRAM buffer, a compute pass converts it into
the (scaled) shared memory, and when the texture is sampled, a load pass
and a copy bring it back into a host texture. In Pirates of the
Caribbean: At World's End the scene is resolved and sampled several
times per frame; at a 2x2 resolution scale the chain was about 2 ms of
the ~10 ms frame.

New cvar d3d12_lazy_resolve (default off). When set, a resolve whose
source is one single-sampled color render target and whose destination
format holds the host data as is (7e3 or 16-bit float to
k_16_16_16_16_FLOAT, 8888 to k_8_8_8_8, 16_16 and 32-bit floats; no
exponent bias, swap or array destination) does not touch the shared
memory: the resolved rectangle is copied into a spare host render target
of the same key (a small pool), and a LazyResolve records the
destination memory range, the texel origin in the destination texture
and what the resolve needs (the dump rectangle and the copy shader
constants). The destination is marked as GPU-written as usual, so
textures over it are invalidated.

- Texture cache: before a texture's shared memory ranges are requested
  (RequestTextures and LoadTextureData), the new virtual
  TryLoadTextureDataFromResolveCache gives the D3D12 implementation a
  chance to fill a tiled 2D texture without mips straight from the
  pending resolves covering it (newest first, so that a newer resolve to
  the same memory overrides older ones; a copy per rectangle, in the
  order the resolves were done), skipping the load entirely.
- Anything else that needs the memory gets the real data first: the
  shared memory calls a new range request callback at the start of
  RequestRanges (vertex and index fetches, memory exports, textures the
  fast path cannot fill), non-lazy resolves to overlapping memory write
  the pending ones before copying, CPU writes to the range (seen through
  a global watch) mark the entry dirty so the resolve lands before the
  CPU data is uploaded, and the pool and the entry count are bounded by
  writing out the oldest. Writing a pending resolve out reuses the dump
  (DumpRectangles, split out of DumpRenderTargets) and the copy pass
  (PerformResolveCopy, split out of Resolve) on the spare render target.
- Readback of resolves must be off: the CPU never sees the data
  otherwise either, so nothing changes for it.

Dock of Port Royal, RTX 4070 Ti, 2x2, no MSAA: GPU 59 % -> 49 % at
60 FPS; the image is the same (also the bloom chain and the luminance
reduction, which go through the fast path). Spare render targets are
never destroyed while the command list may still reference them.

